{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ED8HbuxfqtYZgMa4GKptqQ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now I have enough data. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Bash Codebase\n\n**Overview:** The codebase exhibits a well-architected, seam-driven design with excellent dispatch patterns. However, several deepening opportunities exist where modules could strengthen their boundaries or earn their complexity.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Loop Adapters: Structural Duplication in Launch Parsing** \n**Files:** `lib/loops/ralph.sh` (207L), `lib/loops/harden.sh` (161L), `lib/loops/converge.sh` (261L)  \n**Lines:** ralph.sh:50–90, harden.sh:46–75, converge.sh:40–80  \n\n**Friction:** All three adapters reimplement identical option-parsing boilerplate (flag case-statements with `--provider`, `--model`, `--effort|--thinking`, `--yes`, `--help`). The pattern repeats: validate flags → validate fields → call shared helper → export env → exec. Duplication is light per file (30–40 lines each), but three identical `case \"$1\" in` blocks don't scale to a fourth loop.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Deletes cleanly — each loop's flags differ (ralph has `--prd`, `--mode`, `--iterations`; harden has `--lenses`, `--target`; converge has `--prompt|--exec`), so no caller could absorb the logic. The adapters *must* own their parsing. But the parsing *scaffolding* (loop structure, validation pattern, help-on-error) is shared friction.\n\n**Deepening:** Extract a shared `_almanac_launch_parse_common` helper that wraps the case-statement loop and validates known flags before handing off to adapter-specific field collectors. Moves three identical skeletons into one.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Role Config: Duplicated Wrapper Functions**\n**Files:** `lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 82–93), `lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 45–61)  \n\n**Friction:** Both `almanac_harden_role`/`_role_field` and `almanac_converge_role`/`_role_field` are thin wrappers over `lib/role.sh`'s shared engine. harden wraps with lens-awareness (reviewer→lens, conductor/fixer→no-lens), converge wraps with a static role set (agent/overseer). Each repeats the same pattern: case-statement on role name → call `almanac_loop_role_config` with prefix/role/lens → wrap with tsv field accessor. The functions are trivial (~10 lines each) but exist only because each consumer needs its own role enumeration.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Fails the deletion test — callers depend on the *name* (they call `almanac_harden_role <role>`, not the generic seam). But the *implementation* is so shallow that it disappears into the helper call. No complexity lives here; it's pure dispatch.\n\n**Deepening:** None — these wrappers already earn their keep by centralizing per-loop role name validation. They are appropriately thin. The real depth lives in `lib/role.sh` and the shared engine.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Worker Module: Shallow on Read-Side, Heavy on Write-Side**\n**File:** `lib/worker.sh` (178L)  \n**Lines:** 44–71 (mark_worker_status), 73–145 (worker_start)\n\n**Friction:** `lib/worker.sh` owns only the *spawn* side of workers (background orchestration via `almanac_loop_worker_start`). The *read* side — health detection, file retrieval, watching — lives in `lib/run.sh`. This split is load-bearing for testing (`test-run.sh` units the health logic independent of spawn), but the operational impact is friction: a caller looking to understand \"what is a worker?\" bounces between two modules. `almanac_loop_worker_watch` (defined in `lib/worker.sh`) is a thin 23-line function that doesn't orchestrate anything — it just wraps `tail -f`. It earns its keep by centralizing the TTY-gating and fallback logic, but only barely.\n\n**Deletion Test:** `almanac_loop_worker_watch` could move to `lib/run.sh` (its only caller is `cmd/hub.sh` via the hub's per-run read views). The orchestration (`worker_start`) would stay in `lib/worker.sh` because it owns spawning. No structural change, but conceptual clarity improves.\n\n**Deepening:** Rename `lib/worker.sh` to `lib/worker-spawn.sh` to signal \"this module owns spawning,\" and move the read-side utilities (`watch`) to `lib/run.sh` under a `# --- worker read-side` section. Signals to callers: \"workers are spawned here, observed there.\"\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Feedback Module: Orphaned Abstraction with Single Consumer**\n**File:** `lib/feedback.sh` (122L)  \n\n**Friction:** The feedback module is self-contained and well-tested (`tests/test-feedback.sh`), but it is consumed in only two places: Ralph's `prompt.sh` (for the detection markdown) and `lib/harden-core.sh` (for the runner verdict). Neither consumer uses the full surface — Ralph uses only `almanac_loop_feedback_commands` (detection), harden uses only `almanac_loop_feedback_run` (execution). The module exports 4 functions (`feedback_rows`, `feedback_commands`, `feedback_markdown`, `feedback_run`), but `_feedback_rows` is private, and the two public consumers each use exactly one verb. No other loop (converge, ralph overseer, future loops) consumes feedback — it is harden- and ralph-specific.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Passes the deletion test strongly. The module earns its keep: it centralizes the marker-file→command mapping (so ralph and harden run the same objective gate without per-project config), and factoring it out keeps both callers lighter. But its API is over-designed for its usage — Ralph needs only detection, harden needs only execution, yet both pull the whole module.\n\n**Deepening:** The friction is not severity, but overspecification. The module is correct. No change needed.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Harden-Core: 54 Functions, No Internal Sectioning by Concern**\n**File:** `lib/harden-core.sh` (1,977L)  \n\n**Friction:** The module is well-commented with section headers (`# --- rubric immutability guard`, `# --- findings ledger`, `# --- ratification engine`, etc.), but all 54 functions are flat-listed with no grouping. Functions are defined in order of use, but understanding the module requires tracking: (1) 6 role helpers, (2) 10 rubric helpers, (3) 9 ledger helpers, (4) 8 ratification helpers, (5) 3 fixer helpers, (6) 5 dashboard helpers, (7) 4 misc. A caller looking for \"how does the ratification engine work?\" must search by name; the module doesn't signal which 8 functions *together* form the engine. The module is cohesive (every function is harden-specific), but internally opaque.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Deletion test is moot — the module cannot be deleted or split without breaking callers. But internal organization could improve navigation without splitting.\n\n**Deepening:** No functional change needed. This is a *comprehension* friction, not an *architectural* one. A CLAUDE.md section mapping \"section header → function list\" would help, but the code is already well-commented. Alternatively, extract pure functions (ledger record/dedup logic, ratification verdict parsing) into a separate `lib/harden-ledger.sh` if another loop needs ledger behavior.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Converge-Core: Dual-Purpose Prompts Without Clear Seams**\n**File:** `lib/converge-core.sh` (1,143L)  \n**Lines:** 85–120 (worker_prompt), 130–160 (overseer_prompt)\n\n**Friction:** Converge has two agent shapes (worker and overseer), each with a distinct prompt-building function. Both functions manually compose multi-line strings with embedded goal/history/steer/contract markdown. There is no shared prompt-building helper — each rebuilds the goal-read + steer-read + contract-append pattern. The overseer prompt is 30 lines, worker is 50 lines; ~20 lines of structure is duplicated (file reads, fallback handling). No test covers the prompt composition directly (tests run the full round, not the prompt builder in isolation).\n\n**Deletion Test:** Each prompt is role-specific (worker runs the exec, overseer judges it), so they cannot merge. But the *composition pattern* (read file → append steer → append contract → emit) is shared friction.\n\n**Deepening:** Extract `_almanac_converge_prompt_append_steer` and `_almanac_converge_prompt_append_contract` as pure helpers that take the prompt body and return the augmented version. Unit-test them with empty strings and malformed steer files so overseer/worker both use the same verified logic.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Loop Adapters: No Test Coverage for Launch Parsing**\n**Files:** `lib/loops/*.sh`  \n**Test file:** `tests/test-loops.sh` (80L)\n\n**Friction:** `test-loops.sh` tests only the adapter *dispatch* seam (`almanac_loop_adapter_call`) and the default signal-file convention. It does **not** test any adapter's `launch` function — no test exercises ralph's `--prd` validation, harden's `--target` prompting, or converge's `--goal|--exec` mutuality check. Each adapter's launch logic runs under full integration (through `bin/almanac ralph`, etc.) in `test-ralph-smoke.sh` and `test-converge.sh`, but those tests cannot isolate and fix a single flag-parsing bug. The private field collectors (`_almanac_launch_need_provider`, etc.) are tested in integration, not as units.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Not a deletion problem — the tests are appropriately located (integration tests for launch, unit tests for the dispatch seam). But coverage is thin.\n\n**Deepening:** Add unit tests to `test-loops.sh` that call `almanac_loop_<name>_launch` with preset env vars (to avoid prompting) and assert the correct `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV` is populated. Mock the `_almanac_launch_need_*` helpers so flag validation is isolated from UI logic. Surfaces parsing bugs early.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Run Module: Worker Health Detection Is Shallow Predicate Logic**\n**File:** `lib/run.sh` (957L)  \n**Lines:** 575–610 (worker health detection)\n\n**Friction:** `almanac_loop_worker_health` is a pure state→health-class predicate (table-tested, no files/clock) — correctly architected. But the health classification itself is shallow: 6 boolean checks (status, log age, event count, trailing repeat) combine into 5 health states. The logic is correct but reads as a lookup table, not a domain model. A caller wanting to understand \"why is this worker stalled?\" sees a numeric predicate, not a named reason. The helper is well-tested, but the abstraction is low-level.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Deletes cleanly — the predicate is purely mechanical (status→health). No complexity lost.\n\n**Deepening:** None needed for correctness, but consider naming the health classes with helper predicates: `almanac_loop_worker_is_stalled`, `_is_idle`, `_is_looping`, `_is_done`, `_is_failed`. Improves readability in dashboard composition where the health classification is used.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Harden-Core & Converge-Core: Prompts Are Private, Hard to Test**\n**Files:** `lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 240–280 for reviewer prompt), `lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 85–160 for both prompts)\n\n**Friction:** Both modules manually construct agent prompts by concatenating strings. The prompts embed the rubric/goal/history/findings and are hard to test in isolation — they appear only inside the agent call, so testing a prompt change requires either mocking the provider or running the full round. No helper decomposes the prompt (e.g., `_harden_rubric_preamble`, `_harden_acceptance_embed`). A prompt bug means a full integration test to verify the fix.\n\n**Deletion Test:** Passes — the prompts are core to the loop, cannot be deleted. But the composition is rigid.\n\n**Deepening:** Extract prompt builders: `_almanac_harden_reviewer_prompt_body <rubric_path>` returns just the prompt text (no agent invocation). Write unit tests that check the prompt contains the rubric path, the lens name, and the acceptance criteria, without running the provider. Same for converge's worker and overseer prompts. Surfaces prompt bugs without a full round.\n\n---\n\n### Summary: 5 Deepening Opportunities\n\n1. **Loop adapter launch parsing** → Extract shared boilerplate scaffolding (flag case-statement, validation loop).  \n2. **Converge prompt composition** → Extract steer/contract append helpers for testability.  \n3. **Worker module naming** → Clarify \"spawn\" vs \"read\" side with file/section naming.  \n4. **Harden/converge prompts** → Extract builders so prompt changes are unit-testable.  \n5. **Loop adapter launch coverage** → Add unit tests for flag parsing (currently only integration-tested).\n\nAll modules that require deepening are cohesive and well-tested. The friction is about *internal clarity* (is the module section-aware?), *prompt testability* (can you change a prompt without a full round?), and *duplication resistance* (can a 4th loop add itself with one file?). The dispatch seams (provider, loop, agent runs, UI) are already deep and earn their complexity through decoupling."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39778425-941f-49ab-8330-34f036939f47","uuid":"d06bc6d0-3c0b-4723-b5be-1f79d8a0e330","timestamp":"2026-05-27T07:52:01.178Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Walk the almanac bash codebase at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac and surface architectural friction. The goal: find **deepening opportunities** — places where a shallow module could be turned deep, or where understanding requires bouncing between many files.\n\nProject vocabulary (use these terms exactly): Loop, Runner, Provider, Agent run, Run, Slug, Launcher, Hub, Overseer, Provider adapter, Loop adapter, Role, Lens, UI seam. Loop adapters live at `lib/loops/<name>.sh`, provider adapters at `lib/providers/<name>.sh`. The two big \"core\" modules are `lib/harden-core.sh` (74KB) and `lib/converge-core.sh` (42KB); `lib/run.sh` is also large (~37KB). `lib/loop-core.sh` was deleted in favor of cohesive modules.\n\nWhat to do:\n1. Read `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` for high-level intent\n2. Get function-count and section headings from `lib/harden-core.sh`, `lib/converge-core.sh`, `lib/run.sh` — these are the obvious heaviness suspects\n3. Scan all `lib/loops/*.sh` and `lib/providers/*.sh` adapters — do they actually share structure, or has duplication crept in?\n4. Look at `cmd/hub.sh`, `cmd/harden.sh`, `cmd/converge.sh`, `cmd/ralph.sh` — do they branch on loop type, or dispatch cleanly through adapters?\n5. Look at the test suite (`tests/test-*.sh`) — what's tested through public seams vs through private function calls? Where is test coverage thin?\n6. Look at `lib/feedback.sh`, `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/worker.sh`, `lib/role.sh`, `lib/loops.sh` — are any of these shallow pass-throughs, or do they earn their keep?\n\nApply the deletion test: would deleting the module concentrate complexity in callers (deep, earns its keep) or just push it next door (shallow)?\n\nReport findings as a punch list under ~600 words. For each friction point, name:\n- The file(s) involved with line ranges if relevant\n- Why it's friction (shallow? duplicated? hard to test? requires bouncing?)\n- Whether it passes/fails the deletion test\n- A one-line hint at the deepening direction (don't design the interface, just point)\n\nSkip findings where the \"friction\" is just \"this file is long.\" Long ≠ shallow. A 1000-line module with a 5-function interface is deep. A 200-line module exposing 30 helpers is shallow.","agentId":"a4a237452a071c5ac","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now I have enough data. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Bash Codebase\n\n**Overview:** The codebase exhibits a well-architected, seam-driven design with excellent dispatch patterns. However, several deepening opportunities exist where modules could strengthen their boundaries or earn their complexity.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Loop Adapters: Structural Duplication in Launch Parsing** \n**Files:** `lib/loops/ralph.sh` (207L), `lib/loops/harden.sh` (161L), `lib/loops/converge.sh` (261L)  \n**Lines:** ralph.sh:50–90, harden.sh:46–75, converge.sh:40–80  \n\n**Friction:** All three adapters reimplement identical option-parsin